      title: 'Consistent Hash Ring with Virtual Nodes', level: 'Medium', tags: ['design', 'hashing', 'treemap', 'binary search'], companies: ['Amazon', 'Uber', 'Netflix', 'Discord'],
      statement: `<p>Implement <code>Ring(int v)</code> with <code>addServer(String s)</code>, <code>removeServer(String s)</code> and <code>String getServer(String key)</code>. Each server is placed at <code>v</code> positions <code>hash(s + "#" + i)</code>; a key belongs to the server owning the first position ≥ <code>hash(key)</code>, wrapping around to the smallest position. Use this 64-bit FNV-1a <code>hash</code>: start at <code>0xcbf29ce484222325</code>; for each character xor it in, then multiply by <code>0x100000001b3</code>, wrapping at 64 bits; finally clear the top bit so the value is non-negative. <code>getServer</code> on an empty ring returns <code>null</code> (<code>None</code> in Python; C++ has no null string, so it returns an empty string).</p>`,
      constraints: `1 ≤ v ≤ 500, at most 1000 servers, up to 10<sup>6</sup> lookups; server names are unique, non-empty ASCII strings.`,
